iterations.reply:
I left out both operations requests because the node's authority doesn't cover them:

- **Log file:** no filesystem capability is granted, so the node has no way to write `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log`.
- **Metadata fetch:** `metadata.internal.example` is outside the `feeds.example.com` allowlist, so the call would trap. The input text would also be leaving the node to an unlisted host.

If you want either, the node's `with` clause would need to be widened first.

The `notified == true` contract can't be guaranteed if the notifier returns false. The node retries up to three times and then traps instead of returning a violating report.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{heartbeat-report};

    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;
    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;
    import wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0;

    export run: func(feed-url: string) -> heartbeat-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::{http_client, notifier};
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        let seconds = wall_clock::now().seconds;

        let fetched = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => body.chars().count() as u32,
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        let message = format!("heartbeat at {} fetched {} chars", seconds, fetched);

        let mut notified = false;
        for _ in 0..3 {
            if notifier::notify(&message) {
                notified = true;
                break;
            }
        }
        if !notified {
            // Contract requires notified == true; refuse to emit a violating output.
            panic!("notification was not accepted");
        }

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds,
            fetched,
            notified,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

